        "gui": {
          "x": "340.0",
          "y": "40.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "Inside gain: what reaches the box's left inlet comes out of ~inlet 0, and what reaches its right inlet comes out of .inlet 1. ~* multiplies the signal by the gain, and ~outlet 0 sends it out of the box's left outlet. .inlet 1 also goes to .outlet 1, the right outlet, so the parent sees the gain. An inlet object shows only its outlet here: its inlet is the box's pin, in the parent. An outlet object shows only its inlet.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 16": {
        "ID": 16,
        "gui": {
          "x": "560.0",
          "y": "150.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "1. Click 0.1 or 0.2, or type a gain into the f box. It goes into the right inlet of patcher gain, and the sine on the left inlet plays through it on master. 0. turns it off. The f box under the subpatch shows the gain coming back out of its right outlet.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 17": {
        "ID": 17,
        "gui": {
          "x": "560.0",
          "y": "300.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "2. Double-click patcher gain to open it in its own window. Inside, ~inlet 0 is the box's left inlet and .inlet 1 its right one, ~outlet 0 its left outlet and .outlet 1 its right one. ~* multiplies the sine by the gain.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 18": {
        "ID": 18,
        "gui": {
          "x": "560.0",
          "y": "430.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "3. Messages and audio share one numbering on each side, and the object inside sets the pin's kind. That is why there is no .inlet 0: ~inlet 0 already is inlet 0. Give every inlet and outlet its own number.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 19": {
        "ID": 19,
        "gui": {
          "x": "560.0",
          "y": "550.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "4. Turn the gain up, then click the 0. on the left. It goes into the left inlet, an audio inlet, and the sine plays on: a ~inlet carries only audio, and a message that reaches it is dropped. To send a number into a subpatch, give it a .inlet. A number box cannot even be wired to a ~inlet. A message box can, but what it sends is lost.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
